  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 225 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nThe upper-level trough will continue eastward Saturday night through \nSunday with an accompanying cold front sweeping through the CWA. \nEnsemble model guidance probabilities of measurable rainfall have \nbeen steadily at 90+ percent, with our official forecast (6-hourly) \nPoPs also increasing and peaking between early Sunday morning into \nearly afternoon as confidence increases in the timing of the \nfront. The highest rainfall amounts are forecast to be in \ncentral/northeastern MO and west-central IL with probabilities \nof 0.25\"+ rainfall 50 to 70 percent where frontal convergence \nand large-scale ascent are progged to be strongest. A few \ndeterministic models have up to 250 J/kg of MUCAPE as well, \nsuggesting a rumble or two of thunder is possible. There will be\na west to east gradient in high temperatures on Sunday due to \ndifferences in frontal passage timing, but a much cooler airmass\nwill be arriving behind the front.\n\nNext week, global model guidance is coming into better agreement \nthat a large area of anomalous surface high pressure will dominate \nthe north-central CONUS and hold the seasonably cool airmass with \nbelow average temperatures across the Mid-Mississippi River Valley. \nDuring the second half of the week, global model guidance \nbecomes highly divergent on the timing and evolution of multiple\nnorthern and southern-stream upper-level troughs, including \ntheir timings and how/if they phase with one another. There are \na wide range of scenarios in terms of whether or not there are \nsensible weather impacts in the CWA, but most ensemble model \nmembers currently have either a period of light precipitation \nor dry conditions.",
